## a b s t r a c t
In the photo-Fenton reactions, fast recombination of photoinduced electrons and holes in Fe-based metal–organic frameworks (Fe-MOFs) slows $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ cycle, which remains big challenge that significantly retards the overall process. Herein, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) (NM88) was modified with 3,5- diaminobenzoic acid (DB) and TPB-DMTP-COF (COF-OMe) to in situ construct $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}$ COF-OMe composite that could strongly harvest the visible light for photo-Fenton degradation of sulfamerazine (SMR). With the addition of DB, electron-donating effect of NM88 was strengthened, which then promoted amino groups to react with aldehyde groups (Schiff-base), and thus highly facilitated the interfacial contact between NM88 and COF-OMe. Such modifications increased the degradation rate constants for $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C O I}$ -OMe to 15.1 and 17.3 times that of NM88 and COF-OMe respectively with good reusability. Moreover, the catalyst exhibited 32–170 times higher degradation kinetics in comparison to other reported catalysts. Results showed that due to the Schiff-base reaction between NM88(DB) and COF-OMe, electron density on Fe(III) was decreased; and the photogenerated electrons of COFOMe moved to NM88(DB) to reduce Fe(III), thus resulting in the generation of highly active Fe(II) and OH species. Furthermore, the main reactive species were determined to be OH and $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ by trapping experiments, and a possible mechanism of the degradation system followed Z-scheme charge transfer. $\circledcirc$ 2022 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
## 1. Introduction
Sulfamerazine (SMR) is a broad-spectrum sulfonamide antibiotic to treat various human and animal infectious diseases and is also used in formulated feed in aquaculture [1]. Due to the incomplete biological metabolism and feed residues, SMR remains in the aquatic environment for a long-time, thus creating serious environmental problems [2]. From a recent statistic, various concentrations of SMR have been detected in wastewater, which cannot be effectively removed by the conventional wastewater treatment [3]. Therefore, developing an effective technology to dispose of SMR is urgent.
In recent years, photo-Fenton catalysis has been an important technique for degrading antibiotics in water owing to its mild operating conditions, low energy consumption and high degradation efficiency [4]. Fabrication of photo-Fenton catalyst with high utilization of visible light, excellent photoelectric properties and a high $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ transformation capacity is the core of this technique. Among many Fe-based catalysts, Fe-based metal–organic frameworks (Fe-MOFs) have shown high photo-Fenton degradation performance for organic pollutants in water due to their highly divergent and unsaturated metal sites, and organic ligands with strong optical absorption [5]. However, the photogenerated electrons in Fe-MOFs are susceptible to recombination with holes, limiting $\mathsf{F e}(\mathrm{III})$ transforming into $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ by the available electrons of Fe-MOFs and resulting in poor cycling performance. Some researchers have studied synergy strategy between Fenton reagents and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ or photoelectron from $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ in Fenton-like reaction to solve this problem [6,7].
Covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are a class of porous materials, which are composed of organic building blocks joined together by covalent bonds [8]. This unique structure of COFs not only gives ultra-high chemical stability, but their characteristic groups and the highly ordered conjugation system also improve the absorption utilization rates of visible light [9]. Meanwhile, the mesoporous structure of COFs having a large specific-surface area (SSA) of COFs favors rapid adsorption and diffusion of contaminants. For the above reasons, researchers have attempted to coat the surface of Fe-MOFs with COFs in order to promote the enrichment of target molecules as well as light harvesting and the structural stability of the catalyst [10,11]. For instance, Guo et al. fabricated an $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B/TpPa-1-COF hybrid by utilizing amino groups of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B as anchors to grow TpPa-1-COF, and the hybrid could degrade $86\%$ TC in $40\mathrm{min}$ via the photo-Fenton route, which was about 3 times that of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B [12]. Based on the same strategy, Liu et al. constructed a MIL-101- $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@^{\prime}$ TpMA hybrid that degraded about $88\%$ of BPA in $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ , which was about 1.9 times that of MIL- ${\cdot101-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ , and the degradation rate decreased more than $5\%$ after five cycle runs [13]. From the above reports, coating the surface of MOFs with COFs did enhance the photoFenton degradation performance. However, there are still problems with the long degradation time of organic pollutants and low structural stability. The reason lies in that direct utilization of the amino groups of MOFs will lead to the steric hindrance effects and low electron density of amino groups that impede the growth of COFs. This weakens the covalent binding of amino groups on MOFs to aldehyde groups on COFs, resulting in a weak electron transfer from COFs to MOFs and the low efficiency of the Fe(III)/Fe(II) cycle.
Herein, we in situ constructed NM88(DB) $\lvert_{0.85}\rvert$ COF-OMe by modifying $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) (NM88) with 3,5-diaminobenzoic acid (DB) and TPB-DMTP-COF (COF-OMe) for highly efficient photoFenton degradation of SMR. The physical properties of synthesized composites were investigated by field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and other techniques. Subsequently, the photo-Fenton activity of NM88(DB)0.85/COF-OMe for SMR degradation was evaluated and compared to reported state-of-the-art catalysts. The results obtained from characterization proved that the Schiff-base reaction between NM88(DB) and COF-OMe decreased the electron density of Fe(III), and the photogenerated electrons from COF-OMe accelerated the $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ cycle, leading to a boost in photoFenton catalytic activity. Ultimately, based on Mott-Schottky and electron spin-resonance spectroscopy (ESR) findings, the degradation mechanism was proposed.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Sulfamerazine (SMR, $\geq98.0\%$ ), 1,3,5-tris(4-aminophenyl) benzene (TPB, $293.0\%$ , 3,5-diaminobenzoic acid (DB, $98.0\%$ , ammonium oxalate (AO, $98.0\%$ , $n$ -butanol $(99.0\%)$ and 1,4-dioxane $(\geq99.0\%)$ were obtained from Aladdin Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). 2-aminoterephthalic acid $\mathrm{.H}_{2}\mathsf{A T A}$ , $\geq99.0\%$ and Ferric chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $\geq98.0\%$ were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. 2,5-dimethoxyterephthalaldehyde (DMTP, $\geq98.0\%$ 1 was bought from Energy Chemical Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). N, $N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethyl-formamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ was acquired from Shanghai Shenbo Chemical Co., Ltd. Acetone $(99.0\%)$ was supplied by Chengdu Kelong Chemical Co., Ltd. (Chengdu, China). Methanol $(99.8\%)$ and Tetrahydrofuran (THF, $99.5\%\$ were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). Isopropyl alcohol (IPA, $99.7\%$ was obtained from Guangdong Guanghua Technology Co., Ltd. All the chemicals and reagents were analytically pure, and were used without further purification. Ultrapure water was prepared by using the Smart-S15uv system.
## 2.2. Synthesis procedures of MOF, COF and their composites
Preparation of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe): $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) was synthesized via a previously reported solvothermal process [14]. Briefly, $270~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $180~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{A T A}$ and $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF were mixed in a beaker, and the mixture was stirred for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Subsequently, $0.4~\mathrm{mL}$ of NaOH (2 M) was introduced to the above solution and transferred to an autoclave (Teflon-lined) for solvothermal reaction at $373\mathrm{K}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the mixture was centrifuged and washed with deionized water and acetone several times. Finally, the brown product was collected, which was named as NM88.
Synthesis of DB anchored $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe): To improve the MOF compatibility towards the COF crystal, DB having reacting groups $\left(-\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right)$ with COF was anchored in $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) through carboxyl coordination with metal prior to $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B (Fe) synthesis steps. The synthesis procedure was similar to that of NM88 except for adding the DB amount into the NM88 precursor solution. The adding amount of DB was $15~\mathrm{mg}$ , and the adding ratio of DB to the original ligand $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathsf{A T A)}$ was 1:10. This MOFs was named as NM88(DB).
Synthesis of TPB-DMTP-COF: The TPB-DMTP-COF was synthesized according to the reported procedure [15]. TPB $(21~\mathrm{mg})$ and DMTP $\cdot17\mathrm{~mg})$ were mixed in ${\mathfrak{g}}_{\mathrm{\mathfrak{mL}}}$ mixture of $n$ -butanol-1,4- dioxane-methanol $(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v},\mathsf{4};4{:}1)$ . To the above solution, $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ of aqueous acetic acid $(12\ \mathbf{M})$ was added and then the mixture was hydrothermally treated in a Teflon-lined autoclave at $70~^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After centrifugation centrifuging and washing with THF several times, the yellow product was collected, which was termed as COF-OMe.
Fabrication of NM88(DB)x/COF-OMe: $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})\mathbf{x}/$ COF-OMe was prepared using the same method as mentioned for TPBDMTP-COF, except that a certain amount of NM88(DB) (98, 115, 132, 140 and $148{\mathrm{~mg}}$ , respectively) was added to the COF-OMe precursor solution. The ‘‘X” represented the weight ratio of NM88 (DB) in NM88(DB)x/COF-OMe as 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.85 and 0.9.
Fabrication of $\Nu\Nu88_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe and composites: $\Nu\Nu88_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe was prepared by a similar method as that of NM88x (DB)/COF-OMe, except that NM88(DB) was replaced with $140~\mathrm{mg}$ of NM88.
## 2.3. Characterization
Field emission scanning electron microscope (Hitachi, SU8220) was utilized to analyze the morphology of samples. The microstructure and elemental distribution of samples were examined by using transmission electron microscopy (TEM) (FEI, Titan G2 60–300) couple with energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) at a voltage of $300\mathrm{kV}.$ The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were recorded on a Rigaku SmartLab $(3~\mathrm{kW})$ diffractometer. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas were calculated from $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms measured at $77\mathrm{K}$ on a surface area and pore size analyzer (Micromeritics, ASAP 2460). Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were collected on an FT-IR spectrometer (Nicolet, Nexus670). The elemental surface distribution of the samples was determined with an $\mathsf{X}.$ -ray photoelectron spectrometer (XPS) (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Escalab 250Xi). The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA/DTG) of the samples was measured under $50~\mathrm{mL}$ min $\Nu_{2}$ , from $35~^{\circ}\mathsf C\mathrm{~-~}600~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ at a ramping rate of $10~\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ by employing a thermal gravimetric analyzer (Mettler Toledo $\mathsf{T G A}/$ $\mathsf{D S C3}\mathrm{+}/1100\mathrm{LF})$ . The UV–vis diffused reflectance spectra (UV–vis DRS) were obtained on a Persee TU-1901 spectrophotometer. A Hitachi F-7000 florescence spectrophotometer was used to collect the photoluminescence (PL) spectra. The reactive oxidative species (ROS) was measured on an electron spin resonance (ESR) spectrometer (Bruker, ESP 300E). UV–vis spectroscopy with KSCN as developer was used to measure the leached Fe ions. Electrochemical impedance (EIS), Mott-Schottky (MS), as well as cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were carried out on an electrochemical workstation (Princeton, P4000).
## 2.4. Photo-Fenton activity experiments
The photo-Fenton catalytic activity of the synthesized samples was evaluated by the following procedure: In brief, $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst and $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of SMR solution $(10~\mathrm{mg/L})$ were added into a $250~\mathrm{mL}$ quartz photoreactor, which was stirred for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ in the dark, followed by the addition of $2\ \mathrm{mmol/L}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Then, a 300 W xenon lamp $(\lambda>420\mathrm{nm})$ was used to irradiate the reaction system for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ . At the given intervals, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of the suspension was extracted and filtered by a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ syringe filter to remove the residual catalyst particulates. All experiments were conducted at three times and the results were averaged.
The concentration of SMR was determined on an Agilent 1260 Infinity high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) equipped with a ZORBAX SB C18 column and UV detector. The solution of acetonitrile $(\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}}/\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}})$ $(35\%)$ and water $(65\%)$ was used as the mobile phase with a flow-rate of $1.0\ \mathrm{mL/min.}\ 20\ \upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the sample was injected each time and analyzed at a wavelength of $275\ \mathrm{nm}$ . In order to calculate mineralization ratio of SMR, total organic carbon (TOC) (JENA, Multi $\mathsf{N}/\mathsf C3100)$ analyzer was utilized to obtain TOC content. The degradation ratio of SMR was calculated by the following Eq. (1):
$$
D(\%)=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{t})}{C_{0}}\times100\%
$$
Additionally, the pseudo-first-order model was used to fit the photo-Fenton catalytic reactions, and the kinetic rates of the degradation were calculated by Eq. (2):
$$
\ln\left({\frac{C_{0}}{C_{\mathrm{t}}}}\right)={\mathrm{kt}} 
$$
The intermediates of SMR were identified by HPLC coupled with triple quadrupole mass spectrometry (HPLC-MS) (Thermo Scientific, LTQ-Orbitrap Elite) with a C18 column (ACQUITY UPLC $@$ BEH). The mobile phase in gradient elution detection was a mixture of water $.0.1\%$ formic acid) and methanol. In electrospray ion source (scan range $m/z$ of $50{-}500~\mathrm{Da}^{\cdot}$ , the samples were scanned, and the structures were identified by the software (Mass Hunter Qualitative Analysis).
## 2.5. Theoretical calculations
Calculations using density functional theory (DFT) were performed using the software package (Gaussian 09) [16]. The optimization of the geometric structure and frequency calculation were predicted using the B3LYP functional at the ${6}{-}311\boldsymbol{\mathrm{G}}^{*}$ fundamental level [17]. Gaussian 09 software was used to construct the wavefunction based on the electrostatic potential (ESP) and Hirshfeld charge of DB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ATA}.$
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structure and morphology
The surface morphology of NM88, COF-OMe, NM88(DB), $\Nu\tt M88_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe and NM88(DB)0.85/COF-OMe was determined by FE-SEM and presented in Fig. 1a-e. As depicted in Fig. 1a and b, pristine NM88 nanoparticles were in a spindle shape with an average particle size of $425.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ , while bare COF-OMe possessed a spherical-like structure with average size of $590.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ [18,19]. In order to reduce the steric hindrance and improve the electrondonating effect of amino groups in NM88, we added ligand DB to the synthetic precursor of NM88 in advance and got NM88(DB). As shown in Fig. 1c, the particle size of NM88(DB) decreased, which could be attributed to the unsaturated coordination of Fe ions with ligands [20]. After placing NM88 and NM88(DB) in the precursor solution of COF-OMe for secondary growth, two composites $\mathsf{N M}88_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe and $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe were obtained. The SEM image of NM88(DB)0.85/COF-OMe (Fig. 1e) revealed that COF-OMe particles were uniformly distributed and attached to the surface of the NM88(DB) spindles, while many independent spindles and agglomerated particles were noticed in $\Nu\Nu88_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe (Fig. 1d). This was due to NM88(DB) possessing lower steric hindrance and a stronger electron-donating effect of amino groups than that in NM88, which induced the nucleation of COFOMe on the surface of NM88(DB) [21]. Conversely, COF-OMe preferred to nucleate and grow in the solution when there was NM88 in the reaction system, which was also demonstrated by the particle size distributions of these two composites. To further reveal the microstructure of NM88(DB)/COF-OMe composite, TEM analysis and the corresponding of EDS mapping of $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C O F-}$ OMe were shown in Fig. 1f-j. As shown, it displayed amount of small COF-OMe particles covered on surface of the NM88(DB) spindles with tight connection. The spindle-shaped materials comprised of mostly Fe and O elements, while the extra part consisted of only C and N elements. This result further demonstrated that COF-OMe was highly distributed throughout the surface of NM88(DB).
The PXRD patterns of the MOF/COFs compared with pristine NM88 and COF-OMe are displayed in Fig. 2a. The characteristic peaks of NM88 mainly appeared at $29.17^{\circ}$ (002), $10.41^{\circ}$ (10 1), $18.74^{\circ}$ (200) and $20.96^{\circ}$ (202) [22]. After introducing monodentate carboxylate ligand DB into NM88, ligand vacancy appeared in the crystal, which might have led to the exposure of the crystalline plane and thus intensified the (002) peak [23]. In addition, the (2 0 2) peak slightly shifted toward a lower diffraction angle, which could be attributed to the interplanar spacing was expanded by DB with larger size than original ligand [24]. The diffraction peaks of COF-OMe appeared at $2.75^{\circ}$ , $4.77^{\circ}$ , $5.56^{\circ}$ and $7.38^{\circ}$ belonging to the (1 0 0), (1 1 0), (2 0 0) and (2 1 0) lattice plane, similar to those seen in previous studies [25]. For the $\mathsf{N M}88_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe and $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C O F{-}O M e}$ , the diffraction peaks assigned to NM88 and COF-OMe appeared in their PXRD patterns, suggesting that the structural integrity of NM88 was maintained when combined with COF-OMe. Interestingly, the decreased intensity of the (100) peak was discovered in $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}}/$ COF-OMe, which could be attributed to COF-OMe embedded into the crystal defects of NM88(DB) showing a low diffraction signal. Furthermore, the COF-OMe growth on the defect sites in NM88(DB) was further proved by the shift of (101) and (202) peaks, indicating COF with the larger lattice parameters might lead to interplanar spacing expansion [26]. The intensity and position of PXRD peaks of $\Nu\Nu88_{0.85}/$ /COF-OMe did not change, indicating that NM88 and COF-OMe caused no interference to each other [27].
Fig. 2b showed the surface functionalities of the synthesized samples via FT-IR analysis, where the peaks at around 3331 and $3468~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in NM88 were caused by the amino groups symmetric and asymmetric stretching vibrations; and the peaks positioned at 1577 and $1382~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belonged to the stretching vibrations of the carboxyl groups [28,29]. The $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ stretching vibration, the Fe-O bond stretching vibration and the benzene ring $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bonding vibration were located at 1254, 584 and $769~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [30,31]. In contrast, the absorption bands did not change in the FT-IR spectra of NM88(DB). For COF-OMe, the peaks at 1617 and $1592\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represented the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ and the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ stretching vibration [25]. Additionally, the characteristic peaks $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ appeared in the FT-IR spectra of $\mathsf{N M}88_{0.85}/\mathsf{C O F-O M e}$ and $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C O F{-O M}}$ , suggesting the structural integrity of COF-OMe. It was worth noting that the blue-shift of the amino group absorption band was found in $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C O F{-}O M e}$ , whereas the position of the absorption band in $\Nu\Nu88_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe remained unchanged. This provided evidence for the electrostatic interaction between the amino groups and the aldehyde groups [32], suggesting the electron effect of NM88(DB) could promote the Schiff-base reaction. Therefore, PXRD and FT-IR results initially suggested that the introduction of DB could regulate the electron effects of NM88 and induce aldehyde groups to react with it.
The surface porosity of NM88, NM88(DB), COF-OMe and their composites were determined by measuring $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}.$ As illustrated in Fig. 3a, the two isotherms of NM88 (black) and NM88(DB) (green) were of type III, while the other samples exhibited a type Ⅳ isotherm, which were characteristic of mesoporous materials [33,34]. The specific surface area of NM88 was estimated to be $42.1\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Table 1), and the low porosity of NM88 was due to the breathing structures of the Fe-MOFs having closed pores, which were unavailable for nitrogen adsorption–desorption measurements in a dry form [35]. In comparison, NM88(DB) showed a slightly higher surface area of $49.1\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ which was attributed to the generation of ligand vacancy caused by monodentate carboxylate DB. Because of the presence of COFOMe with a large surface area $(2162.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ , the surface areas of $\mathsf{N M}88_{0.85}/\mathsf{C O F}$ -OMe and $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C O l}$ F-OMe increased significantly. Among which, the higher surface area in $\mathrm{NM88}(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe might be attributed to the ligand vacancy that reduced steric hindrance in NM88 and promoted COF-OMe to grow on MOFs [36]. Additionally, as expected, the experimental surface area of $\mathsf{N M}88_{0.85}$ COF-OMe was close to the theoretical value evaluated according to mass proportion, suggesting that COF-OMe grew independently rather than further assembled based on MOFs in this system. The pore size distributions of these samples were enumerated by density functional theory (DFT) method, and the results were shown in Fig. S1. Differed from NM880.85/COF-OMe, a new pore size of $2.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ was found in $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/\mathsf{C O F}–\mathsf{O M e}.$ which could be the evidence for the presence of interaction between them.
The thermal stability of NM88, NM88(DB), COF-OMe, $\Nu\Nu88_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe and NM88(DB)0.85/COF-OMe was investigated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), and the results were displayed in Fig. 3b. From TGA and DTG curves, NM88 and NM88(DB) showed three mass loss stages, where the first two stages below $320^{\circ}\mathsf C$ could be attributed to absorbed water on the surface and chemically bound water removal, respectively [26]. In the step from 320 to $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ the mass loss was explained by the decomposition of organic linkers and the collapse of the skeleton [5]. Moreover, NM88(DB) collapsed at a similar temperature to NM88, indicating the introduction of monodentate carboxylate DB would not destroy the thermal stability. For COF-OMe, it exhibited good stability and would not decompose until $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ [37]. In common with NM88, the TGA/DTG curves of $\mathsf{N M}88_{0.85_{i}}$ /COF-OMe and NM88 $(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe showed three mass losses. Furthermore, the collapsing temperature of the two samples was increased to $464~^{\circ}C$ , and the mass loss was decreased to varying degrees. Among them, $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C(}$ OF-OMe possessed a higher residue weight, suggesting its high thermal durability, due to the intimate interaction of MOFs and COFs.
The chemical state and chemical composition of the obtained samples were investigated by XPS. The XPS survey spectra presented in Fig. 4a confirmed that C, N, O and Fe elements were found in all the corresponding samples except COF-OMe only contained C, N and O elements. As shown in Table S1, NM88 contained approximately 6.90 (at. $\%$ ) N element and 4.82 (at. $\%$ ) Fe. After the amount of amino groups was increased by introducing DB into NM88, the N element content increased to 7.60 (at. $\%$ ). For COFOMe, it was composed of more than 82 (at. $\%$ ) of the C element. Both $\Nu\Nu88_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe and $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C O}$ F-OMe exhibited a significant decrease in Fe element content in comparison with NM88, indicating the presence of COF-OMe. Furthermore, the N element content of $\mathrm{NM}88(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/($ COF-OMe was lower that could be explained by the presence of more aldehyde containing monomers at the contact interface between NM88(DB) and COF-OMe. In contrast, more amino containing monomers were exposed in $\Nu\tt M88_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe because of the independent COF-OMe.
To further investigate the contribution underlying the effect of introducing DB into NM88 on the growth of COF-OMe, the Fe 2p, O 1 s and N 1 s spectra of different samples were analyzed in Fig. 4- b-d. First, Fig. 4b presented the high-resolution Fe $\mathsf{2p}$ spectra of NM88, NM88(DB), NM880.85/COF-OMe and $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C O F-}$
OMe. The Fe 2p spectrum of NM88 was deconvoluted into four peaks, where the peaks for Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ were located at 725.6 and $712.0\ \mathrm{~eV}$ , respectively. Their corresponding satellite peaks were at 730.3 and $716.9\mathrm{~eV}$ respectively, implying the existence of Fe(III) in NM88 [38]. All the peaks in the Fe $2{\tt p}$ spectrum of NM88(DB) showed a decrease in binding energy compared to NM88, indicating the electron density on Fe(III) was increased. This could be attributed to the stronger electron-donating effect of amino groups in DB than in $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{A T A}$ , which increased the electron density of the carboxyl groups and resulted in a decrease in the binding energy of Fe(III) in Fe-O bond formed as a result of coordination [39]. Interestingly, these peaks in $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe were shifted to higher binding energies than NM88, implying that the electron density on Fe(III) was significantly decreased, which might be related to the reaction occurred on the amino groups of NM88(DB). Since the reaction between amino groups and aldehyde groups (Schiff-base) followed the nucleophilic addition mechanism, during which amine served as the nucleophile to attack the aldehyde, the electrons on the N atom of amino groups were donated to the C atom of aldehyde groups. Actually, apart from the electrons of amino groups in NM88(DB), the electrons on Fe (III) were utilized to conduct the Schiff-base reaction, which decreased the electron density on Fe(III). Moreover, $\Nu\Nu88_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe showed no distinct change in binding energy, which suggested electron density on Fe(III) was not influenced.
From the high-resolution O 1 s spectra in Fig. 4c, the corresponding binding energy peaks of NM88 at 530.4, 531.7 and $532.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ were belonged to the Fe-O, $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ and ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-O}}({\mathsf{H}})$ groups of the organic linkers, respectively [40]. In the O 1 s spectra of NM88(DB), the binding energy of the Fe-O peak was increased, which was due to the electrons on the O atom being transferred to Fe(III) under the influence of the electron-donating effect of amino groups. For COF-OMe, the peaks located at 532.7 and $531.7\mathrm{eV}$ were attributed to the $_{C-{\cal{O}}(\mathsf{C})}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ groups from aldehyde groups and methoxy groups, respectively [41]. Additionally, the binding energy of Fe-O peak shifted from 530.5 to $530.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ in $\mathsf{N M88}(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/\mathsf{C O F{-}O M e}$ , which could be attributed to the Schiff-base reaction between NM88(DB) and COF-OMe.
The N 1 s core-level peaks of the samples are displayed in Fig. 4d, and the peaks of NM88 at 399.0 and $400.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ were attributed to the CAN bond and $\boldsymbol{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ from organic linkers, respectively [22]. The binding energy of the $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ peak of NM88(DB) increased by about $0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which was caused by the electrons on N being donated to Fe(III). For the COF-OMe, apart from the peaks at 399.0 and $400.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ bond and $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ , the peak at $399.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ was assigned to the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ bond derived from the condensation of the amino groups and aldehyde groups [42]. Remarkably, the $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ peak of $\mathrm{NM}88(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe was further shifted to high binding energy, indicating the interaction between NM88(DB) and COF-OMe [43]. This along with the change of binding energy in Fe 2p and O 1 s spectra, confirmed that NM88(DB)
could donate more electrons to aldehyde containing monomers, resulting in a lower electron density on Fe(III) in $\mathrm{NM88}(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe.
Furthermore, the electron-donating effect of the amino groups of (A) ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{ATA}}$ and (B) DB was assessed by DFT calculation, and their ESP mapping was displayed in Fig. 4e. The electrostatic potential followed the color order of red $\angle{\cdot}$ orange $\prec$ yellow $\prec$ green $\prec$ blue, where the red region showed the highest electron density, so electron-donating behavior was easier to happen. As predicted, the amino groups on $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{A T A}$ exhibited green while those on DB turned yellow, indicating the enhanced electron-donating effect of the amino groups. According to the Hammett constants $(\upsigma)$ of substituent, the electrophilicity of the aldehyde group $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\cdot}\sigma=0.35$ ) was much higher than that of the amino group $\upsigma=-0.16$ [44], suggesting that COF-OMe had a strong electron-withdrawing effect on NM88(DB). Therefore, the introduction of DB to the NM88/COFOMe composite could improve electron-donating effect of NM88.
## 3.2. Photocatalytic activity and photostability
To study the effects of different photo-Fenton conditions on this system, the presence or absence of visible light, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and catalyst were controlled. As depicted in Fig. 5c, the self-decomposition of SMR was negligible under visible light irradiation. Under the condition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with visible light, negligible degradation of SMR was observed, revealing that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ could not degrade SMR directly, even activated by visible light. When $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or visible light was excluded, the SMR degradation rate was lower than $19\%$ , which indicated that neither the photocatalytic reaction nor the Fenton reaction dominated the SMR degradation. However, in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and catalyst, SMR could be completely degraded within $30\mathrm{min}$ under visible light irradiation. Therefore, the excellent degradation performance of $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe was mainly from the photo-Fenton reaction.
Fig. 5d depicts the effect of the mass ratio in $\mathrm{NM88(DB)x/COF-}$ OMe on the photo-Fenton degradation of SMR. The fractions (by weights) of NM88(DB) and COF-OMe in different $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})\mathbf{x}/$ COF-OMe composites was shown in Table S2. It could be seen that the SMR adsorption performance of composites was weakened with the increased content of NM88(DB) from $60\%$ to $90\%$ due to the poor adsorption capacity of NM88(DB). In the same situation, the variation in SMR degradation performance followed a bellshaped trend, with NM88(DB)x/COF-OMe containing $85\%$ NM88 (DB) exhibiting the highest photo-Fenton catalytic activity. This could be attributed to that an appropriate amount of NM88(DB) was favorable to the integration with COF-OMe, which adjusted the electron density on Fe(III) subsequently. Then, the content excess of NM88(DB) might prevent COF-OMe harvesting light and thus reduced the photo-Fenton degradation performance [46]. Fig. 5e shows the effect of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration from 0.5 to $4.0\ \mathrm{\mmol/L}$ on the photo-Fenton degradation reaction. The SMR degradation efficiency was enhanced with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration from 0.5 to $2.0\ \mathrm{mmol/L}$ , which was due to more OH radicals being generated by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [45]. When the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration further was increased to $4.0\ \mathrm{\mmol/L}$ , the SMR degradation rate decreased from $100\%$ to $86.8\%$ after $30\mathrm{min}$ , which could be attributed to the removal of OH radicals by the excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Eq (3) and (4)). Therefore, $2.0\ \mathrm{mmol/L}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was used in the following experiment.
$$
H_{2}O_{2}+\cdot O H\rightarrow H_{2}O+H O_{2}.
$$
$$
H O_{2}\cdot+\cdot O H\rightarrow H_{2}O+O_{2}
$$
The effect of pH value from 3.0 to 11.0 on the photo-Fenton degradation performance of $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe was displayed in Fig. 5f. Obviously, the SMR adsorption kinetic was largest under neutral $\mathsf{p H}$ , while the other pH conditions exhibited a decrease trend. As reported, $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe and SMR possessed the same kind of charge under acidic and alkaline pH, the electrostatic attraction was strongest under neutral pH, which led to the highest adsorption kinetic [34]. For degradation reaction, the kinetics was relatively higher at pH from 3.0 to 6.2, while at higher $\mathsf{p H}$ it showed a significant decrease trend. Affected by adsorption reaction, the degradation kinetic of the catalyst was slightly decreased under acidic condition. When at a higher pH, the concentration of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ was decreased with the pH value changed from 11.0 to 6.2, which promoted the resolution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and resulted in an increasing catalytic activity. Hence, the catalyst with great degradation performance under neutral pH has potential in practical applications.
To evaluate the stability of $\mathrm{NM}88(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe, the photoFenton SMR degradation experiments were recycled 10 times under the same condition. As depicted in Fig. 6a, the degradation performance of $\mathrm{NM}88(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/\$ COF-OMe showed almost no decrease after 10 cycles, which indicated the outstanding performance stability of the sample. In addition, the crystal structure stability of $\mathrm{NM88}(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/($ COF-Ome was investigated by its PXRD pattern after 10 cycles. From Fig. 6b, a slight decrease in the intensity of some peaks from NM88(DB) was observed, which was due to the ‘‘breathing effect” of NM88(DB) [47]. The high stability of NM88(DB)0.85/COF-OMe could be attributed to the enhanced reduction of Fe(III) to Fe(II), which resulted in the generation of more Fe(II) for multiple applications. The Fe ions leached rate was detected during three consecutive cycles of $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe, and the result was shown in Table S3. Almost no Fe ions was leached, which indicated that the degradation reaction probably occurred at the heterogeneous interface rather than in homogeneous solution.
In order to verify the presence of ${\mathrm{Fe}}({\mathrm{II}}).$ , XPS spectra of NM88 $(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/\mathrm{COF-OM}\epsilon$ e before and after degradation were investigated (Fig. 6c). There was no signal of $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ in fresh $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C O F}.$ OMe, while the ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{II})$ peak appeared at $710.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ in used NM88 $(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe [48]. This suggests that the $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ redox cycle was accelerated, resulting in more Fe(II). Gao et al. suggested that in Fenton-like reactions using MIL-88B-X as a catalyst, the reduction of Fe(III) could be promoted due to the decreased electron density of Fe(III) by increasing substituent electrophilicity [49]. Similarly, we proposed that replacing amino groups in NM88(DB) with COF-OMe increased substituent electrophilicity, which might lead to a decrease in electron density on $\mathsf{F e}(\mathrm{III})$ and facilitate $\mathsf{F e}(\mathrm{III})$ reduction. Therefore, both the degradation performance and stability of $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C}$ OF-OMe were effectively enhanced, which suggested its vast potential in the actual water treatment.
In addition, the degradation performance for sulfadiazine (SDZ), sulfamethazine (SMT), sulfamethoxazole (SMX) or SMR over different recently reported catalysts is provided in Table 2. Compared with other systems in the table, our reaction system needed a much lower dosage of catalyst, which was $4-10\%$ of the others. When their degradation kinetics at unit dosage of catalyst and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ were compared, the $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe prepared in this study had the highest degradation rate constant, which was 32–170 times higher than these reported catalysts.
## 3.3. Optical and electronic properties
$$
\left(\alpha h\nu\right)^{1/n}=A(h\nu-E g)
$$
where A is a constant, $\alpha$ is the absorption coefficient, hv is the photon energy, and $\mathfrak{n}$ is determined by the type of optical transition of the samples [4]. As presented in Fig. 7b, the $E\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}$ of NM88, NM88(DB), COF-OMe, $\Nu\tt M88_{0.85}/\$ /COF-OMe and $\mathrm{NM88}(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe were estimated to be 1.78, 1.72, 2.49, 2.47 and $1.80\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. It was obvious that the $E\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}$ of $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C O F-}$ OMe was much smaller than that of $\Nu\tt M88_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe, indicating that COF-OMe improved the visible light response of NM88(DB).
The PL spectra were used for analyzing the photogenerated carrier recombination rate, and the PL spectra of different samples are shown in Fig. 7c. Generally, lower PL emission peak intensities implied a lower recombination rate of the photogenerated carriers, which led to higher photocatalytic activity [4]. It could be seen that NM88 showed an intense emission peak at about $450~\mathrm{nm}$ , which could be ascribed to the recombination of photogenerated carriers. The PL intensity of NM88(DB) was decreased due to the excitation of amino groups, which made excited electrons transfer to Fe-O clusters [59]. For COF-OMe, several emission peaks appeared in the range of 42 $)\ -520\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Notably, the peak at about $520~\mathrm{nm}$ was not observed in the spectra of $\mathrm{NM}88(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe, which might be attributed to the excited electrons in COF-OMe being transferred through inter-molecules [60]. Furthermore, the intensity of PL emission peaks of $\mathsf{V M}88(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe was significantly reduced, revealing that the recombination of photogenerated carriers was greatly inhibited and further suggesting the electron transfer between NM88(DB) and COF-OMe [61]. In comparison, the slight decrease in PL peak intensity and the presence of the peak at around $520\mathrm{nm}$ in $\Nu\tt M88_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe indicated that the excited electrons in COF-OMe were mainly recombined with holes.
The efficiency of charge transfer in these samples was determined by using electrochemical-impedance spectroscopy (EIS). From the EIS Nyquist-plots in Fig. 7d, the charge transfer resistances of NM88, NM88(DB), COF-OMe, $\mathsf{N M}88_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe and NM88(DB)0.85/COF-OMe were 426.8, 301.4, 588.7, 349.5 and $120.4\ \Omega$ , respectively. Obviously, the introduction of DB caused NM88(DB)0.85/COF-OMe to have the lowest charge transfer resistance, resulting in an improve electron transfer efficiency. Moreover, the redox performance of the samples was studied by cyclic voltammetry (CV) and their CV curves are shown in Fig. S5. It could be seen that NM88(DB) $\lvert_{0.85}\rvert$ COF-OMe had the smallest redox potential difference $(\Delta E_{\mathrm{p}})$ and the highest peak current $\left(I_{\mathrm{p}}\right)$ , indicating that $\mathsf{F e}(\mathrm{III})$ could be reduced more easily to Fe(II) [48]. Therefore, the enhanced photo-Fenton catalytic activity of NM88 $(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe could be because of the fast conversion of Fe(III).
## 3.4. Degradation mechanism and pathway
The possible mechanism of SMR photo-Fenton degradation for $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C}$ OF-OMe was investigated by reactive species trapping experiments and electron spin resonance (ESR) spectra. In the trapping experiments, the holes $(h^{+})_{\ast}$ superoxide radical $(\cdot0_{2}^{-})$ , hydroxyl radical (OH) and electrons $(e^{-})$ were trapped by isopropanol (IPA, $1\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ , nitrogen $(\mathsf{N}_{2})$ , ammonium oxalate (AO, $1\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ and silver nitrate $(\mathsf{A g N O}_{3}$ , $1\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ , respectively [62]. It could be observed from Fig. 8a that the $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe degradation efficiency for SMR decreased from $100\%$ to $48\%$ with the addition of IPA. Meanwhile, the degradation rate declined to $74\%89\%$ and $93\%$ under the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , AO and ${\sf A g N O}_{3}$ environment, respectively. The result revealed that OH was the main active species in the photo-Fenton degradation system, while $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ played a minor role.
To further probe the ability of NM88, NM88(DB) and NM88 $(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe to produce OH and $\cdot0_{2}^{-},$ , an ESR spin-trapping experiment using 5,5-dimethyl-1-pyrroline $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -oxide (DMPO) as the trapping agent was conducted (Fig. 8b-c and Fig. S6). As displayed in Fig. S6a, the ESR curves of the two catalysts did not show any signal in the dark, while the characteristic DMPO- OH adduct quartet peak (intensity ratio: 1:2:2:1) were seen in light irradiation (Fig. 8b). This meant OH could not be generated in the absence of visible light. Moreover, the peak intensity of DMPO- OH of NM88 $(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}$ /COF-OMe was 3.7 and 5.6 times that of NM88(DB) and NM88, respectively. Similarly, there was no signal under the dark in Fig. S6b, while the corresponding DMPO- $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ adduct sextet peaks were seen in the presence of light (Fig. 8c). The peak intensity of DMPO- $.0_{2}$ of $\mathsf{N M}88(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe was 2.0 and 2.3 times that of NM88(DB) and NM88, respectively. This finding was in accordance with the results obtained from reactive species trapping experiments, which further suggested that OH and $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ served as the major and secondary active species in the degradation system, respectively. Combined with the results that pure photocatalytic and Fenton reaction showed relative low activity (Fig. 5c), the degradation mechanism was likely synergetic effect of photocatalysis and Fenton process of the designed catalyst, which needed to be validated in the following experiments.
In order to analyze the electronic band structure of NM88(DB) and COF-OMe, the Mott-Schottky measurement was conducted (Fig. 8d and e). These two materials possessed $n$ -type semiconductor character due to their positive slope [63]. The flat-band potentials $\left(E_{\mathbf{fb}}\right)$ of them were determined to be $-0.50$ and $-0.12{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. SCE, respectively. On the basis of equation $(E({\mathrm{NHE}})\ =\ E$ $(\mathsf{S C E})+0.24)$ , their electrode potentials were $-0.26$ and 0.12 eV vs. NHE. Since $n$ -type semiconductor conduction band $(E_{\mathrm{CB}})$ has been reported to be $\sim0.1$ eV lower compared to its $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{fb}}$ in general [64], the $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ of NM88(DB) and COF-OMe were estimated to be $-0.36$ and $0.02\ \mathrm{eV}$ vs. NHE, respectively. Combined with the $E\mathbf{g}$ and based on the empirical formula: $:\mathrm{{E}_{\mathrm{{g}}}=\mathrm{{\E}_{\mathrm{{VB}}}-\mathrm{{E}_{\mathrm{{CB}}}}}}$ , the valence bands $(E_{\nabla\ B})$ of the samples were calculated to be 1.36 and 2.51 eV vs. NHE, respectively.
Based on the above experiments and characterization result analysis, the possible mechanism of photo-Fenton degradation of SMR in the $\mathsf{N M88}(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}/\mathsf{C O F}–\mathsf{O M e}/\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}/$ visible light system was proposed (Fig. 9). The Fe-O clusters of Fe-MOFs were excited by visible light and photogenerated electrons could transfer to metal sites, leading to the conversion of $\mathsf{F e}(\mathrm{III})$ to $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ (Eq (8)) [59]. Meanwhile, COFs could generate electron-hole pair under visible light illumination and the electrons from the CB of COF-OMe could be transferred to the VB of NM88(DB) for recombination with the holes. Consequently, more electrons accumulated at the CB of NM88(DB) to provide available electrons for accelerating the reduction of Fe(III), while holes accumulated at the VB of COF-OMe. Because the $E_{\mathsf{C B}}$ of NM88(DB) (-0.36 eV vs. NHE) is reported to be negative in comparison to $0_{2}/\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ standard redoxpotential $\left(-0.33\ \mathrm{eV}\right.$ vs. NHE), the electrons on the NM88(DB) CB could oxidize $0_{2}$ to produce $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ (Eq (9)) [65]. Moreover, the holes on the VB of COF-OMe could react with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to produce OH (Eq (10)), since the $\scriptstyle{E_{\mathrm{VB}}}$ of COF-OMe (2.51 eV vs. NHE) was more positive than the $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}/$ OH standard redox-potential $2.40\:\mathrm{eV}$ vs. NHE) [58]. Notably, pure NM88(DB) and COF-OMe exhibited poor photo-Fenton degradation performance, suggesting $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ generated in NM88(DB) and OH in COF-OMe contributed less to the degradation reaction. In addition to the optical excitation mechanism described above, the decreased electron density of Fe(III) aided in the accelerating the conversion of Fe(III), which was the major contributor to boost the photo-Fenton catalytic activity. Therefore, more $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ generated in the reaction and was oxidized by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , resulting in the production of more OH (Eq (11)). Finally, the SMR was subsequently degraded into small molecules by the generated OH and $0_{2}^{-}$ (Eq (12) and (13)).
