Based on these finding discussed above, the possible degradation pathway of SMR in the $\mathsf{N M88}(\mathsf{D B})_{0.85}/\mathsf{C O F}–\mathsf{O M e}/\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ system was proposed in Fig. 10 and Fig. S7. There were three possible degradation pathways of SMR: (1) The generated OH and $0_{2}^{-}$ firstly oxidized the methyl group to carboxyl group, which transformed SMR to S1 $(m/z~=~295.0)$ . Meanwhile, these radicals attacked $S{\mathrm{-N}}$ bond of SMR to get S2 $(m/z=110.1)$ [66]. Then, the $S{\mathrm{-}}\mathbb{N}$ bond in S1 was broken or S2 was oxidized, leading to form S5 $(m/z=140.0)$ [67]. Finally, the rings of S5 were broken to generate S8 $(m/z=151.0)$ , which was further oxidized to small organic acids even ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ [68]; (2) The SAN bond in SMR was broken to produce S3 $(m/z=174.0)$ , and then its amino group was oxidized to nitro group to get S6 ${\mathrm{'}m}/z=204.1{\mathrm{'}}$ . The rings of S6 were also broken to S8 and the following steps were the same as pathway 1; (3) SMR was demethylated firstly to produce S4 $(m/z=251.1)$ , which was followed by $S0_{2}$ extrusion to generate S7 $(m/z=187.1\$ . After hydroxylation, S7 was transformed to S9 $(m/z=219.1)$ and further mineralized to small molecules.
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ cycle was accelerated the by enhancing electronic effect in synthesized $\mathrm{NM}88(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/\mathrm{\Omega}$ COF-OMe and the composite was then employed to boost photo-Fenton degradation of SMR. Many MOFs/COFs composites have been applied in degrading organic pollutants [12,13], but long degradation time and low stability caused by weak connection limited their application. The construction of $\mathrm{NM88(DB)_{0.85}/C O F{-O M e}}$ could solve these: the Schiff-base reaction reduced the electron density on Fe (III), and COF-OMe coating on the surface generated electrons with optical excitation, both of which promoted the conversion of Fe(III) to reduced form Fe(II) and therefore enhanced the photo-Fenton catalytic activity. The composite exhibited almost complete degradation of SMR in 30 min under visible light with a degradation rate constant of $0.1056\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , which was 15.1 and 17.3 times that of NM88 and COF-OMe, respectively. Simultaneously, the degradation efficiency of $\mathrm{NM88}(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85}/$ COF-OMe showed no obvious change after 10 reuses, reflecting its good structural stability. Notably, when compared with other reported catalysts in this field [14,50-56], the catalyst had 32–170 times the degradation kinetics at same unit dosage of catalyst and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Furthermore, $.0_{2}^{-}$ and OH were the dominant reactive species, and their proportion in NM88 (DB)0.85/COF-OMe were 5.6 and 2.3 times that of NM88. The possible mechanism of the photo-Fenton SMR degradation of NM88 $(\mathrm{DB})_{0.85},$ /COF-OMe followed a direct Z-scheme charge transfer, with the $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ redox cycle playing a predominant role. Hence, this work provided novel insights into how to enhance photoFenton catalytic activity of Fe-MOFs/COFs composite by accelerating Fe(III)/Fe(II) redox cycle.
